      "heading": "The data pipeline is most of the work",
      "narration": "If you take one thing from this lesson, take this: at scale, data work is not preparation for the real project. It is the real project. Teams that report the largest quality gains almost always got them from the corpus rather than from the architecture, and the architecture has barely changed in years. Four stages. Collection. The bulk of the text comes from Common Crawl, an open snapshot of a large fraction of the public web, or from cleaned derivatives of it. But raw web text alone makes a noticeably worse model than a mixture, so labs add books, source code, Wikipedia, academic papers, and selected forums and discussion sites. The reason mixture helps is that these sources fail in different ways: the web is broad and shallow and full of junk, books are long-form and coherent but old and narrow, code is precisely structured, papers are dense and correct. Together they cover things none of them covers alone. Filtering. Most of what you collect is not text you want a model to imitate. Navigation menus, cookie banners, SEO word salad, auto-generated listings, low-quality machine translation, and pages that are almost entirely markup. The standard approach layers cheap heuristics — document length, punctuation ratios, fraction of lines ending in a full stop, presence of banned tokens — with a small learned classifier trained to recognize text that looks like a reference corpus. The proportion discarded is startling to people who have not seen it: over ninety percent of raw Common Crawl typically does not survive. Deduplication, and this is the step that is both the most valuable and the most often skipped in smaller efforts. The web is enormously redundant — the same article appears on hundreds of sites, the same licence text on millions of pages, the same boilerplate in every template. If a passage appears five thousand times, the model does not learn the general pattern behind it, it memorizes the passage. Exact deduplication is easy; near-duplicate detection, usually with MinHash, is what actually matters. Removing near-duplicates improves quality and shortens training, which is a rare combination and the reason it is the first thing to do. Then mixing, which is a set of deliberate proportion choices rather than a default. One finding worth knowing because it is counter-intuitive: including a substantial fraction of source code improves performance on reasoning tasks that have nothing to do with programming, apparently because code is text with rigorous, checkable structure. And finally contamination, which is a discipline rather than a stage. If your benchmark's test questions are somewhere in your training data — and on a web-scale corpus they very likely are, because benchmarks get posted and discussed — then your evaluation is measuring recall rather than ability. Decontamination means actively searching the corpus for your evaluation sets and removing them, and a model card that does not mention doing it has told you something about how much to trust its numbers.",
