      "heading": "Scaling laws: what Chinchilla actually found",
      "narration": "This is one of the few places in deep learning with a genuinely predictive quantitative theory, and it is worth understanding because it drove billions of dollars of decisions, including one large mistake. The empirical finding is this: test loss decreases as a smooth power law in three quantities — the number of parameters, the number of training tokens, and the total compute. Plot loss against any of them on log axes and you get a remarkably straight line, over many orders of magnitude, with no bumps and no plateau in the range anyone has measured. That smoothness is the useful part. It means you can run a series of small, cheap experiments, fit the curve, and extrapolate to predict what a run a thousand times larger will achieve — before you spend the money. Very little else in this field lets you do that. Now the mistake, because it is instructive. In twenty twenty, the Kaplan scaling laws were read as saying that when you get more compute, you should spend most of it on making the model bigger, and comparatively little on more data. The industry took that seriously, and it is why GPT-3 has a hundred and seventy-five billion parameters and was trained on only three hundred billion tokens. That is under two tokens per parameter. In twenty twenty-two, DeepMind redid the experiment more carefully — over four hundred training runs, sweeping model size and token count against a fixed compute budget — and found the earlier conclusion was wrong. The optimal allocation is to scale parameters and tokens roughly together, at about twenty tokens per parameter. They demonstrated it directly: Chinchilla, at seventy billion parameters trained on one point four trillion tokens, using the same compute as the much larger Gopher, beat it on essentially every benchmark. Most of the models trained in the preceding two years were substantially undertrained for their size. Now the twist that makes this more than a historical correction, and it is the part that actually shapes what you can run today. Compute-optimal means optimal for the training run. But a model is trained once and then serves inference for years, and inference cost is set by the number of parameters — not by how many tokens it was trained on. Training a smaller model for much longer costs more up front and then is cheaper on every single call, forever. So once you account for deployment, the right thing to do is deliberately overshoot Chinchilla: take a seven-billion or seventy-billion parameter model and train it on far more tokens than compute-optimality would suggest. That is precisely what Llama and the open-weight families that followed did — trained well past the optimal point on purpose — and it is the direct reason you can run a genuinely capable model on one consumer GPU. The scaling law told the field what was efficient to train. Economics told it what was worth deploying, and the two answers are different.",
