      "narration": "Two engineering techniques before the punchline, because they are the ones that decide what is possible on the hardware you have. Mixed precision. Thirty-two-bit floats are more precision than neural network arithmetic needs, and they cost you memory and bandwidth for nothing. So you run the matrix multiplications in sixteen bits while keeping a thirty-two-bit master copy of the weights for the optimizer update, where the small increments genuinely do need the precision. There are two sixteen-bit formats and the difference matters. Standard half precision has a narrow exponent range, so small gradients underflow to zero and you need loss scaling — multiplying the loss by a large constant before the backward pass and dividing it out afterwards — to keep them representable. Brain float sixteen instead keeps the same exponent range as thirty-two-bit and gives up mantissa bits, which for gradients is the right trade, and it removes the loss-scaling machinery entirely. Every modern accelerator supports it and it is the default now. Second, gradient checkpointing. During the forward pass you normally store every activation because backward needs them, and on a deep model those activations are most of your memory. Instead, store only a few, and recompute the rest during the backward pass from the nearest stored point. You pay roughly thirty percent more compute and you get a large fraction of your memory back, which very often is the difference between a batch size that trains and one that does not fit. Now the punchline, and it is the most important sentence in the lesson. What comes out at the end of all of this — the data pipeline, the fleet, the months of compute — is a base model. It is an extraordinarily good next-token predictor and it is not an assistant. It has no notion of being asked a question, no concept of a helpful answer, no idea that there is a user. Ask a base model what the capital of Nepal is and a perfectly reasonable continuation is another question, and then another, because lists of questions are a thing that exists on the web and your text looked like the start of one. Ask it for advice and you may get a plausible forum thread including the replies. It is not being unhelpful. Helpfulness was never in the objective; the objective was to predict what text follows, and it is doing that correctly. So there is a second stage, and the encouraging thing is that it is very much cheaper than the first. Instruction tuning teaches the format of a request and a response, using a comparatively small set of curated examples. Preference alignment then teaches which of two valid responses a person would rather have. Together they cost a tiny fraction of pretraining and they are the entire difference between a text predictor and something you can talk to. Pretraining buys capability. Alignment buys behaviour. They are separate stages, they fail in separate ways, and the next two lessons are about the second one.",
